\chapter{Open-access lookup}
\label{unpaywall-module}

Knowing that a paper exists is not the same as being able to read it.  The
claim-backing workflow (\cref{pdf-module}) needs the full text, and a
systematic reviewer wants to know up front which of a session's papers can be
read without a subscription.  Search providers answer this unevenly: OpenAlex
and Semantic Scholar return a PDF link when they have one, while DBLP, IEEE,
Web of Science, and Scopus records carry none---so a paper found through those
providers looks paywalled even when a legal open-access copy exists.

This chapter asks the question directly of Unpaywall, a database that maps a
DOI to its open-access (OA) locations: the publisher's own copy when the
article is gold or bronze OA, and repository copies (arXiv, PubMed~Central,
institutional archives) when it is green.  One lookup per DOI tells us whether
the paper is OA at all, \emph{how} it is OA, and where the best copy lives.

\section{Design overview}

The lookup is one HTTP request per paper: [[GET
https://api.unpaywall.org/v2/{doi}?email=...]].  Unpaywall requires the
[[email]] parameter---a request without it is rejected with HTTP~422---so,
unlike the polite pools of OpenAlex and Crossref where an email merely speeds
things up, here it is a precondition.  We take it from the shared
[[contact_email]] resolver (\cref{sec:contact-email}) and treat a missing
address as \enquote{not configured}: lookups become a silent no-op rather than
a stream of 422 errors, and the CLI tells the user once which variable to set.
We deliberately do \emph{not} fall back to a placeholder address; Unpaywall's
terms ask for a real contact, and a fake one would let the tool appear to work
while violating them.

From the response we keep a small, stable subset:
\begin{description}
\item[ [[is_oa]] and [[oa_status]] ] Whether any OA copy exists, and
  Unpaywall's classification of it: \enquote{gold} (OA journal),
  \enquote{hybrid} (OA article in a subscription journal), \enquote{bronze}
  (free on the publisher site but without a licence), \enquote{green}
  (repository copy only), or \enquote{closed}.
\item[ [[best_oa_location]] ] Unpaywall's ranking of the best copy: its
  landing page [[url]], its direct [[url_for_pdf]] when one is known, the
  [[host_type]] (publisher or repository), the [[version]]
  (published, accepted, or submitted), and the [[license]].
\item[ [[oa_locations]] ] Every known copy, which we scan only as a fallback
  for a PDF link when the best location has none.
\end{description}

Unpaywall is a per-DOI lookup, not a search engine: its title-search endpoint
is limited and its data is the same OA data OpenAlex already exposes.  So this
module is \emph{not} a search provider.  Like the Crossref currency check
(\cref{crossref-module}), it is an enrichment step that annotates papers we
already hold, and it is structured the same way so a reader who knows one
knows the other:
\begin{description}
\item[Caching] Results are cached per DOI through the shared cache layer, so
  enriching a session twice or re-opening a PDF costs nothing.
\item[Silent failure] Network and parse errors are logged and turned into
  \enquote{could not determine}, matching the provider contract that callers
  never wrap lookups in [[try]].
\end{description}

As with Crossref, \enquote{checked, closed} must not be confused with
\enquote{could not check}.  Only an HTTP~200 means we saw the record; a 404
(DOI unknown to Unpaywall), a rate limit, or a transport error says nothing
about the paper's openness.  We return a result only for the former and
[[None]] for the latter, so a transient failure never marks a paper closed.

