\section{The module-level interface}

Callers want a handful of verbs over a single shared client: ask whether
lookups are possible at all, look up one DOI, annotate a paper or a batch, and
resolve a DOI straight to a PDF link.  We instantiate the client once at import
time so its cache and session are shared across the whole process.

<<module-level oa interface>>=
_client = UnpaywallClient()


def is_configured() -> bool:
    """Whether a contact email is set, i.e. lookups will be attempted."""
    return _client.is_configured()


def fetch_oa(doi: str | None) -> dict | None:
    """Look up open-access metadata for a single DOI (see UnpaywallClient)."""
    return _client.fetch_oa(doi)
@

\subsection{Annotating a paper}

The single-paper annotator is what enrichment calls (\cref{enrich-module}).
It writes the lookup onto a copy of the paper via [[replace]] and leaves the
paper untouched when the lookup is undetermined, so a paper without a DOI stays
unchecked and a transient failure never erases a status a previous run had
established.

The one asymmetry is [[pdf_url]]: a link the paper already carries is kept in
preference to Unpaywall's.  The provider that supplied it (OpenAlex, Semantic
Scholar, arXiv) is a source the user has seen and chosen; silently swapping it
for a different host would make the session's record drift from what was
found.  Unpaywall fills the gap only where there is one.

<<module-level oa interface>>=
def annotate_paper(paper: Paper) -> Paper:
    """Return a copy of ``paper`` annotated with Unpaywall open-access data.

    Sets ``oa_status`` and ``oa_url``, and fills ``pdf_url`` if the paper
    has none. A paper whose DOI cannot be resolved (missing, not found, no
    contact email, or a failed request) is returned unchanged.
    """
    result = _client.fetch_oa(paper.doi)
    if result is None:
        return paper
    return replace(
        paper,
        oa_status=result["oa_status"],
        oa_url=result["oa_url"],
        pdf_url=paper.pdf_url or result["pdf_url"],
    )
@

